    <section id="points" class="docs__section">
      <p class="docs__num mono">01</p>
      <h2>Spend your ten points</h2>
      <p>Your points are your deck's thesis. Before you look at a single card, decide which of three things your deck needs most, because that's what points buy:</p>
      <dl class="rules-table">
        <div><dt>Raw power</dt><dd>Cards that are simply better than anything else at their job. Black Lotus, Time Walk, Ancestral Recall, the Moxen, Mana Crypt.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Consistency</dt><dd>Tutors that turn your deck into a smaller, more predictable one. Demonic, Vampiric, Mystical and Enlightened Tutor, Imperial Seal, Merchant Scroll.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>An engine</dt><dd>The single card your combo is built around. Flash, Time Vault, Underworld Breach, Tinker, Tolarian Academy, Thassa's Oracle.</dd></div>
      </dl>
      <p>A combo deck should spend on its engine and the tutors to find it. A midrange deck usually wants raw power it can cast early. An aggro deck often wants very little, because its best cards are already free.</p>

      <h3>Five ways players actually spend ten</h3>
      <p>All of these come from real, current lists in the <a href="/decks/">deck rotation</a>.</p>
      <div class="spends">
        <div class="spend">
          <p class="spend__name">The Mox package</p>
          <p class="spend__math mono">Mox Emerald 3 · Mox Jet 3 · Mox Ruby 3 · Reanimate 1</p>
          <p>Three free mana sources in your colours. Explosive openings and a lot of turn-two four-drops. The Moxen get worse the longer the game goes, so this suits decks that want to be ahead early.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="spend">
          <p class="spend__name">One bomb, then change</p>
          <p class="spend__math mono">Ancestral Recall 8 · Tamiyo 1 · Psychic Frog 1</p>
          <p>The single best card-draw spell ever printed, plus two efficient threats. Or Time Walk at five with five one-pointers alongside it.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="spend">
          <p class="spend__name">The engine and its tutors</p>
          <p class="spend__math mono">Flash 7 · Vampiric Tutor 2 · Imperial Seal 1</p>
          <p>Flash Hulk pays for the combo piece and the two cheapest ways to find it. Nothing else matters if the engine shows up.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="spend">
          <p class="spend__name">Artifact acceleration</p>
          <p class="spend__math mono">Mana Crypt 5 · Sol Ring 3 · Ancient Tomb 1 · Strip Mine 1</p>
          <p>Ramp straight into large threats a turn or two early. Gruul and colourless decks love this.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="spend">
          <p class="spend__name">Spread the ones</p>
          <p class="spend__math mono">Sol Ring 3 · Mox Pearl 3 · Ancient Tomb · Urza's Saga · Strip Mine · White Plume Adventurer</p>
          <p>Two fast-mana pieces, then four one-pointers that are each among the best cards a deck can play. More good draws, fewer games decided by a single card.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Two questions to ask about every pointed card</h3>
      <p><b>What does it do when I draw it on turn six?</b> Black Lotus wins games on turn one and is a blank late. Moxen fade the same way. Tutors and card advantage stay good. Spend on the cards that are strong when your deck needs them.</p>
      <p><b>Would I play it at zero?</b> If the answer is “only because it's famous,” skip it. The list is priced on what cards do in this format, not on their reputation. Timetwister is in the Power Nine and costs nothing. Ancestral Recall costs more than Black Lotus.</p>
      {{ quote(quotes.pay) }}

