    <section id="plan" class="docs__section">
      <p class="docs__num mono">02</p>
      <h2>Pick a plan</h2>
      <p>Singleton punishes decks that try to do everything. Pick one way to win, then make sure most of your hundred cards push toward it. These are the families you'll meet at a Canlander table.</p>
      <div class="archetypes">
        {%- for a in archetypes %}
        <article class="arch">
          <div class="arch__art" style="background-image:url('{{ a.art | cardImg('art_crop') }}')" role="img" aria-label="{{ a.art }} art"></div>
          <div class="arch__body">
            <p class="arch__tag mono">{{ a.tag }}</p>
            <h3 class="arch__name">{{ a.name }}</h3>
            <p>{{ a.how }}</p>
            <p class="arch__meta"><span class="mono">Points</span> {{ a.pts }}</p>
            <p class="arch__meta"><span class="mono">Look for</span> {{ a.keys }}</p>
          </div>
        </article>
        {%- endfor %}
      </div>
      <p>Not sure which one is you? Play a game with three of them on Cockatrice. The one you keep thinking about afterwards is your deck.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="mana" class="docs__section">
      <p class="docs__num mono">03</p>
      <h2>Build the mana</h2>
      <h3>You need fewer lands than you think</h3>
      <p>New players bring Commander instincts and register 36 to 40 lands. Competitive Canlander decks play far fewer, because the games are one-on-one, curves are low, and free cantrips like Brainstorm, Ponder and Preordain dig for land drops.</p>
      <div class="stat">
        <p class="stat__big">32–36</p>
        <p class="stat__label">Lands in the {{ decks.decks.length }} current lists in our deck rotation. The median is 33, plus one to three modal double-faced spells that can be played as lands.</p>
      </div>
      <p>A sensible starting point is <b>34 lands</b> for midrange, <b>32–33</b> for low-curve aggro and tempo, and <b>35–36</b> for control, ramp and decks with expensive top ends. If you run three or more fast-mana pieces (Moxen, Sol Ring, Mana Crypt, Ancient Tomb), shave a land.</p>
      <h3>Use the best lands, but respect the hate</h3>
      <p>A hundred-card deck has room for every fetchland, original dual and shockland in your colours, and you should play them. Fetches also feed Wrenn and Six, delirium and landfall. But Blood Moon, Back to Basics, Wasteland and Strip Mine are all legal and all played. Run a few basics of your main colour, and fetch them early when you can afford to.</p>
      <h3>Two or three colours beats four</h3>
      <p>Four- and five-colour piles have the best card quality and the worst mana. The council has said it wants to <a href="https://canadianhighlander.ca/2025/03/26/2025-year-start-points-update-survey-report/">“promote lower color decks over four color decks”</a>, and the points list has been nudged that way. For a first deck, two colours with a light splash is the most forgiving choice.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="hundred" class="docs__section">
      <p class="docs__num mono">04</p>
      <h2>Fill the hundred</h2>
      <h3>Build in jobs, not cards</h3>
      <p>You can't play four Lightning Bolts. You can play Lightning Bolt, Chain Lightning, Burst Lightning, Fireblast and Lava Dart. Singleton consistency comes from functional copies. List the jobs your deck needs done, then make sure each job has enough bodies that you'll draw one when it matters.</p>
      <div class="skeleton">
        <p class="skeleton__title mono">A starting skeleton for midrange</p>
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Job</th><th>Slots</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Lands</td><td>33–35</td><td>Fetches, duals, shocks, a few basics, utility lands</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Cheap interaction</td><td>14–18</td><td>Swords to Plowshares, Lightning Bolt, Fatal Push, Counterspell, Thoughtseize</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Card advantage &amp; selection</td><td>8–12</td><td>Brainstorm, Ponder, Fact or Fiction, Fable of the Mirror-Breaker</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Threats</td><td>20–25</td><td>Ragavan, Psychic Frog, Orcish Bowmasters, Murktide Regent</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Flexible answers &amp; hate</td><td>6–10</td><td>Kolaghan's Command, Abrupt Decay, Council's Judgment, Force of Will</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p class="skeleton__note">Ranges, not rules. Aggro trades card advantage for more threats; control does the opposite.</p>
      </div>
      <h3>There's no sideboard, so every card plays every matchup</h3>
      <p>Narrow hate cards are dead in half your games. Prefer answers that cover many problems: Kolaghan's Command, Abrupt Decay, Assassin's Trophy, Council's Judgment, Prismatic Ending. Force of Will costs zero points and answers anything. Play a dedicated hate card only if it also does something your deck wants anyway, or the local metagame is full of one strategy.</p>
      <h3>Keep the curve low</h3>
      <p>A one-on-one game at twenty life rewards tempo. Most competitive lists keep the bulk of their spells at one to three mana and only a handful above four. Expensive cards need to win the game or replace themselves.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="tune" class="docs__section">
      <p class="docs__num mono">05</p>
      <h2>Tune it</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Build it on Moxfield.</b> Its Canadian Highlander format totals your points and flags an illegal list for you.</li>
        <li><b>Goldfish opening hands.</b> Draw twenty seven-card hands. If you'd mulligan more than a few, fix the mana before anything else.</li>
        <li><b>Play twenty games on Cockatrice.</b> Keep a list of every card you were unhappy to draw. Those are your first cuts.</li>
        <li><b>Change ten cards at a time</b>, not one. Single swaps are too small to notice in a hundred-card deck.</li>
        <li><b>Watch the points calendar.</b> Updates land roughly every quarter. A list that was legal in August can be eleven points in September.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="checklist" class="docs__section">
      <p class="docs__num mono">06</p>
      <h2>Before you sleeve up</h2>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li>Exactly 100 cards, one of each, basics excepted.</li>
        <li>Ten points or fewer on <a href="{{ site.official.points }}">today's list</a>.</li>
        <li>Your points serve the plan, not the other way round.</li>
        <li>32–36 lands, adjusted for fast mana and curve.</li>
        <li>At least fourteen pieces of cheap interaction.</li>
        <li>A few basics, in case of Blood Moon.</li>
        <li>Every card is live against every deck.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Then go play. The deck will tell you what's wrong with it faster than any guide can.</p>
    </section>

  </article>
